<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samsu-iluna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1749–1712 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samsu-iluna was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>. Shortly after his accession he faced a major rebellion of southern cities led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rim-sin-ii">Rim-Sin II</a>, which he crushed at the cost of destroying the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a>. His reign also saw the breakaway of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sealand-dynasty">Sealand Dynasty</a> in the south, halving the empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna.jpg" alt="Samsu-iluna" width="312" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Budge, E. A. Wallis (Ernest Alfred Wallis), Sir, 1857-1934; King, L. W. (Leonard William), 1869-1919 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Record_of_the_sale_of_land_in_the_reign_of_Samsu-Iluna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Samsu-iluna was the seventh king of the founding Amorite dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>. His name is Amorite and means &quot;The Sun is our god&quot;. When Hammurabi died he had conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, reduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> status and weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, so that Babylon was master of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. These states were weakened but not destroyed. Within a few years of Hammurabi's death Elam and Assyria had left Babylon's orbit and revolts had begun in the conquered territories. Dealing with them fell to Samsu-iluna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In his ninth year a man calling himself Rim-Sin, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rim-sin-ii">Rim-Sin II</a> and perhaps a nephew of the Rim-Sin who had opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>, raised a rebellion at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> that spread to some 26 cities, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a>, Kisurra and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a>. Samsu-iluna dealt the coalition a blow within a year, and the king of Eshnunna, Ilūni, was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and executed by strangulation. After four years of fighting along the borderlands he pulled down the walls of Ur and sacked it, did the same at Uruk and Isin, and defeated Larsa, where Rim-Sin II was killed.</p><p>A few years later a pretender named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilum-ma-ili">Ilum-ma-ili</a>, claiming descent from the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a>, led another revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>. One battle was indecisive and a second went his way, and he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> of Sea-Land, which held Sumer for some 300 years. By Samsu-iluna's death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a> recognised Ilum-ma-ili as king. Kuturnahunte I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> entered the wall-less <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and plundered it, taking a statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a> that was returned only under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>, eleven centuries later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> also regained its independence under a native dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Samsu-iluna turned back a Kassite army in his ninth year, the earliest known mention of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassites">Kassites</a>. He also destroyed Apum, killed its king Yakun-ashar, and built the fortress of Dur-samsuiluna to hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> after a second rebellion. He rebuilt the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a>, restored temples and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurats</a>, and promoted the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> as his father had. Some scholars speculate that he introduced the Standard Babylonian calendar. Records at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> largely stop after his tenth year, and the archaeological evidence points to long abandonment of these southern cities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samsu-iluna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>About a decade after Hammurabi's death in 1750 BCE, his son <b>Samsu-iluna</b> faced a widespread revolt across the south, led by a pretender styling himself Rim-Sin II and joined, according to Samsu-iluna's own inscriptions, by 26 rebel kings.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 19th century BCE, under Rim-Sin I, Hammurabi of Babylon conquered the city, and in the reign of <b>Samsu-iluna</b> it was abandoned.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Uruk joined the revolt against <b>Samsu-iluna of Babylon</b> and suffered decline in the later eighteenth century, yet its temples were later restored and it remained a religious and scholarly centre under the Neo-Babylonian, Achaemenid, Seleucid and Parthian empires.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The throne stayed in one Amorite family for three centuries and normally passed from father to son: Hammurabi succeeded his father Sin-muballit in 1792 BCE, and his son <b>Samsu-iluna</b> succeeded him.</blockquote><a href="/societies/old-babylonia">Social Hierarchy of Old Babylonia</a></li><li><blockquote>The Babylonian Empire under <b>Samsu-iluna</b>, Hammurabi's son, sacked Shubat-Enlil in 1728 BCE and ended the kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/apum">Apum</a></li><li><blockquote>Southern resentment surfaced later in revolts under Hammurabi's son <b>Samsu-iluna</b>, and the far south soon broke away under the Sealand dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/larsa">Larsa</a></li></ul></section>
